    "next"
  ],
  "text": "With real data, you don't know the parameters. You estimate them from the sample. Hand these five hundred measurements to a normal fit, and it returns a mean of seventy-one point nine, with a spread of five point seven five. Now the model answers questions the raw data can't, like a ninety-fifth percentile of eighty-one point four. But a fit ALWAYS returns numbers, even for the wrong shape. Getting an answer doesn't mean it fits.\n\nSo look. A Q-Q plot lines up the data's quantiles against the model's. When the model is right, the points fall on the line. Fit a normal to skewed data, and they bend away at the ends... right where the tails are, and where the costly mistakes live.\n\nEven a good fit only knows where its data was. These four hundred values run from about thirty-five to a hundred and seventeen. Above a hundred? The model says four percent, and there's data there to back it up. Above two hundred? It says zero point... then twenty-three zeros, then a three. Nothing out there was ever observed. That number is the assumed shape, talking. And a fit can only be as good as the data behind it... which depends on how the sample was drawn.",
